# T38409 — GVRP - basic functionality — tb470 IE520 stack — 2026-09-29 — PASS

Verdict: PASS. Both case steps demonstrated with the DUT = IE520 stack (u5) and sw1 = IE520-sa (u3),
GVRP on the non-LAG 1G SX link stack port4.0.26 <-> SA port1.0.26:
- DUT PROPAGATES: the SA created VLANs 3991 and 3992 DYNAMICALLY on port1.0.26(t), both declared by
  the DUT (run 1, 14:43:24), and later VLAN 302 as well (run 2, 14:53:24).
- DUT LEARNS: the DUT created VLAN 301 DYNAMICALLY on port4.0.26(t), registrar INN, declared by the SA
  (run 2, 14:53:15). It also added port4.0.26 as a GVRP-registered tagged member of its existing static
  VLANs 3993/3994/10 (run 1, 14:43:12, `Dynamic Vlans : 10 3993 3994`).
Run labelled: clean. The log was written after the run by a successor bench-runner, from the evidence
files the first tester left (it ran out of usage at ~14:57, after teardown and before logging). The
sentinel had watched the console transcripts live and confirms the same facts.

One OBSERVATION for Terrence (outside the case text, not graded): see "Observation O-1" below. A VLAN
that is created, or that gets its first live member, AFTER GVRP is running on the port was not declared
in 30 s, on either IE520, until GVRP on the port was restarted (`no gvrp` / `gvrp`).

Operator: bench-runner subagent (first tester, 14:41-14:56; this log by its successor at 17:1x),
sentinel = parent session, Terrence away. Queue: IE520/CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md row 7 (VLAN group).
Case text: ck.db AWPTCM-T38409 (2 steps, no expected results written):
  objective: "Check DUT can learn VLAN's from a GVRP server. Check DUT can propogate VLAN's to other switches"
  precondition: "Setup -- sw1 -- DUT --"
