## Step 2 — DUT learns from, and propagates to, the other GVRP switch: PASS
### Run 1, part A (evidence/38409a.out, views after 30 s)
SA 14:43:24 `show vlan dynamic` — the SA LEARNED the DUT's VLANs:
  3991    VLAN3991        DYNAMIC ACTIVE  port1.0.26(t)
  3992    VLAN3992        DYNAMIC ACTIVE  port1.0.26(t)
SA `show gvrp machine` port1.0.26: vlan 3991 and 3992 registrar INN (registered from the peer),
applicant VO (the SA does not re-declare them back); 1, 10, 3993, 3994 registrar INR (static on
both ends). The DUT's `show gvrp statistics` port4.0.26: RX JoinEmpty 6 / JoinIn 27, TX JoinEmpty 40 /
JoinIn 35: PDUs flow both ways.
DUT 14:43:07 `show vlan dynamic` -> empty. Nothing new to learn at this point: the SA's only non-common
VLAN, 301, had its member on SA port1.0.1, which is not cabled, so the SA did not declare it (SA
applicant for 301 absent from `show gvrp machine`). The DUT did add port4.0.26 as a tagged member of
the static VLANs the SA declares: `show vlan brief` 3993/3994 `port4.0.26(t)`, and
`show interface switchport` port4.0.26 `Dynamic Vlans : 10 3993 3994`.
(`show interface port4.0.26 switchport` -> `% Invalid input` at 14:43:13/14:43:28, a wrong show form in
a read-only view, no state effect; the script was not in stop-on-error mode for views.)

### Run 1, part B (evidence/38409b.out) — live members added AFTER GVRP was up
14:44:30 SA port1.0.2 (live, to tb470 eth2) -> access vlan 301; 14:44:42 DUT port1.0.9 (live, to the
x230) -> access vlan 302 (so 3992 lost its only member on the DUT).
After 30 s (14:45:23-14:45:36): DUT `show vlan dynamic` empty; SA dynamic still exactly 3991, 3992.
Neither side declared the new VLAN, and the DUT still declared 3992 (applicant QA) although 3992 no
longer had a member (O-1).
14:45:43 SA port1.0.2 withdrawn (`no switchport access vlan`); 30 s later no change on the DUT.
Teardown run 1 (14:46:32-14:46:54, all DUT teardown lines accepted): the next view,
`show gvrp configuration`, answered `% GVRP is not globally enabled`. That is the correct answer once
GVRP is off, but `--stop-on-error` stopped the script on it, so the SA teardown was finished at 14:48:17
(evidence/38409-sa-teardown.out: `% GVRP is not globally enabled`, no dynamic VLANs, port1.0.26 back to
`switchport mode access`, no gvrp line in the running config). The DUT was read back at 14:48:49
(evidence/38409-dut-after.out: no dynamic VLANs, 3991-3994 on their legs, 1.0.13/4.0.9 connected,
port4.0.26 `switchport mode access`, no gvrp line).

### Run 2 (evidence/38409c.sh + 38409c.out) — pins the learning direction and O-1
14:49:58-14:50:19 GVRP up on both, with no test VLANs yet (DUT legs 1.0.13/4.0.9 shut again).
Baseline 14:50:46-14:50:55: SA dynamic 3991, 3992 again (propagation reproduced); DUT dynamic empty.
TEST A 14:51:02-14:51:26: SA `vlan 301` + live port1.0.2; DUT `vlan 302` + live port1.0.9. After 30 s
(14:52:03-14:52:15) there was NO new dynamic VLAN on either side (O-1 reproduced).
TEST B 14:52:22-14:52:38: `interface port4.0.26 / no gvrp / gvrp` on the DUT, then the same on SA
port1.0.26. After 30 s:
  DUT 14:53:15  show vlan dynamic
    301     VLAN0301        DYNAMIC ACTIVE  port4.0.26(t)          <- the DUT LEARNED 301 from sw1
  DUT gvrp machine: vlan 301 registrar INN (applicant VO); vlan 302 applicant QA (declared); vlan 3992
    applicant VO (no longer declared: it has no member)
  SA  14:53:24  show vlan dynamic
    302     VLAN0302        DYNAMIC ACTIVE  port1.0.26(t)          <- sw1 learned the DUT's 302
    3991    VLAN3991        DYNAMIC ACTIVE  port1.0.26(t)
  SA gvrp machine: 302 registrar INN; 3992 registrar MT (withdrawn by the DUT, so the SA removed its
    dynamic 3992); 301 applicant QA (declared).
TEST C 14:53:34-14:53:54: a global restart on both (`no gvrp enable` / `gvrp enable` /
`gvrp dynamic-vlan-creation` / port `gvrp`). After 30 s (14:54:31-14:54:49) the same state:
DUT dynamic 301; SA dynamic 302 and 3991; `show gvrp configuration` Enabled/Normal 20/60/1000 on both.

Step 2 verdict per direction:
  propagate (other switch learns from the DUT): PASS — SA dynamic 3991/3992 (run 1 and run 2 baseline),
    302 (run 2 TEST B/C), and the SA dropped 3992 when the DUT withdrew it.
  learn (DUT learns from the other switch): PASS — DUT dynamic VLAN 301 on port4.0.26(t), registrar INN
    (run 2 TEST B/C), plus GVRP-registered trunk membership of static 3993/3994/10 (run 1).
Not tested (not asked by the case): traffic forwarding across a dynamically learned VLAN.

## Observation O-1 (for Terrence — not graded; possible defect or by design)
On awplus_main-20260923-20, on both IE520s (DUT stack and the SA), the set of VLANs a GVRP port declares
seemed to be fixed when GVRP came up on the port. It was not refreshed when a VLAN was created or gained its
first live member later (seen three times: run 1 part B, run 2 TEST A, and for 3992 losing its member in
run 1 part B), although LeaveAll is 1000 cs (10 s) and each wait was 30 s. `no gvrp` / `gvrp` on the port
made the new set take effect at once. Whether the leave/leaveall cycle should re-declare it, and whether
the other end then registers it, is a GVRP-behaviour question for the feature owner. It does not
change this case's verdict, which asks only whether learning and propagation work.

